Realtime
========

Socket.IO runs on the same HTTP server as the REST API, at the API origin
without the /api suffix. If the API is http://host:8000/api the socket URL is
http://host:8000.


Handshake and rooms
-------------------

The client passes the access token in the handshake auth field. The server
verifies it with the same function REST uses, so an expired access token means
a rejected socket. On connect the socket joins two rooms:

	user:<userId>          exactly one connection group per account
	students | admins      by the role claim in the token

Controllers never touch the io object. They import emit from
src/realtime/io.ts and use one of:

	emit.toUser(userId, event, payload)
	emit.toAllStudents(event, payload)
	emit.toAllAdmins(event, payload)
	emit.toEveryone(event, payload)


Event contract
--------------

Payloads are deliberately thin. An event says what changed, not what the new
state is; the receiver refetches. This keeps the socket layer from becoming a
second, drifting copy of the API.

	Event                     Audience    Emitted by
	----------------------------------------------------------------
	challenge:created         students    createChallenge
	challenge:deleted         students    deleteChallenge
	challenge:joined          admins      registerForChallenge
	challenge:participants    everyone    registerForChallenge
	validation:submitted      admins      submitChallengePoints
	validation:decided        the user    approve / reject
	validation:resolved       admins      approve / reject
	quests:updated            students    setDailyQuests, clearDailyOverride
	quests:pointsgranted      students    claimQuestPoints
	areas:updated             students    area and sub-activity mutations
	exercise:logged           user+admins logExercise
	checkin:done              the user    checkIn


Who listens to what
-------------------

Admin console (lancer-admin):
	App shell        validation:submitted, validation:resolved (pending badge)
	Validations      validation:submitted, validation:resolved
	ChallengeDetail  validation:resolved, validation:submitted,
	                 challenge:participants
	Leaderboards     validation:resolved, challenge:created
	Quests           quests:updated

Mobile app (lancer-fit_mobile), via src/services/realtime.js:
	challenge:created      refetch challenges and raise a local banner
	validation:submitted   banner
	validation:decided     banner and refetch


Rules for adding an event
-------------------------

1. Name it noun:verb-in-past-tense, lower case, colon separated.
2. Emit it from the controller, after the transaction commits, never inside
   the service layer.
3. Send identifiers, not entities.
4. Address the narrowest room that works. quests:pointsgranted currently goes
   to all students for a single user's claim; that is a bug, not a pattern to
   copy.
5. Add a row to the table above.


Known behaviour to be aware of
------------------------------

* The mobile socket is created after the auth context mounts, so listeners
  registered at mount time can bind to a null socket. src/services/realtime.js
  rebinds once the connection exists. Any new mobile listener must go through
  that hook rather than reaching for the socket directly.
* There is no acknowledgement, retry or replay. A client that was offline when
  an event fired will not see it; it recovers only on its next fetch. Screens
  that matter should still load their data on focus.
* Push notifications are a separate mechanism (Expo push via
  src/utils/push.ts) and are sent alongside, not instead of, socket events.
  Sockets update an open app; push reaches a closed one.